WEBVTT
TITLE Complexity Classes P Np Np Complete — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A decision problem asks one question and expects yes or no.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
P holds the problems a machine decides in polynomial time.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Two to the n is not a polynomial, and it is what brute force costs.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Four assignments out of eight are yes instances of this formula.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
NP means a short certificate can be checked quickly.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Four clauses, three variables, twelve literal slots in all.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Certificate one, zero, zero satisfies all four clauses.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Exactly four of the eight assignments are accepted.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Any decider becomes a verifier that ignores the certificate.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Three different certificates, one unchanged answer.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
So P is contained in NP, in one direction only.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The open question: is P equal to NP?

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
NP-hard: every problem in NP reduces to it.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
NP-hard says nothing about being in NP.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
NP-complete: NP-hard and in NP, both conditions.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
SAT and 3-SAT are NP-complete: the Cook-Levin theorem.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A reduces to B when one fast function preserves yes and no.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Hardness travels along the arrow, from source to target.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
So hardness is proved by reducing a known problem to the candidate.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Membership travels backwards, against the arrow.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
One row of three vertices per clause: twelve vertices.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Join two vertices when they are compatible: 42 edges.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
A clique of size four picks one true literal per clause.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Twelve cliques of size four; the largest clique is four.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Read a clique and you get a satisfying assignment.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Read an assignment and you get a clique of size four.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
An unsatisfiable instance: eight clauses, no assignment.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Its largest clique is seven: one short of the clause count.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
The complement graph keeps exactly the missing edges.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
A clique of size k is a cover of size n minus k.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Here the largest clique is three and the cover is two.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Checked on all 1088 graphs with four or five vertices.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
2-SAT is in P, and 3-SAT is not known to be.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
On 3060 formulas the component test never disagreed.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Clique with k fixed at three is in P as well.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
NP-hard is wider than NP-complete.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
First decide which of the three claims is being made.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Membership: a verifier, or a reduction into a known member.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Hardness: reduce a known NP-complete problem to yours.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
One polynomial algorithm for one NP-complete problem collapses all three.

